 8)<br /></em>Tyree Nesbitt: <em>Until Next Time (2025)<br /></em>Borne/Me
 ylan/Roth: <em>Fantaisie brillante on Themes from Bizet's "Carmen" (1880)
 <br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br /><strong>Ot
 is Murphy</strong> is professor of saxophone at the Indiana University Ja
 cobs School of Music\, where he joined the faculty at age 28\, becoming o
 ne of the youngest professors in the school's history. Recognized interna
 tionally for his artistry\, he has performed in more than 25 countries ac
 ross four continents\, appearing on distinguished stages including Carneg
 ie Hall and Merkin Hall in New York\, Casals Hall in Tokyo\, and the Pala
 u de la Música in Valencia. Murphy is a laureate in several national and
  international competitions\, including the Adolphe Sax International Sax
 ophone Competition and the Jean‑Marie Londeix International Saxophone C
 ompetition. His discography includes numerous acclaimed recordings\, most
  notably his contribution to Pacifica Quartet's <em>Contemporary Voices</
 em>\, featuring Ellen Taaffe Zwilich's Quintet for Alto Saxophone and Str
 ing Quartet\, which won a Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music/Small Ensem
 ble Performance. Internationally respected as a pedagogue\, Murphy has le
 d guest teaching engagements and master classes at major institutions suc
 h as the Paris Conservatory\, the Amsterdam Conservatory\, the Cologne Ac
 ademy of Music\, the Royal College of Music in London\, and the Hamamatsu
  International Wind Instrument Academy. His students have garnered signif
 icant recognition\, earning awards in major competitions and holding prof
 essional appointments worldwide as performers\, educators\, and members o
 f premier military ensembles. Murphy lives in Bloomington\, Indiana\, wit
 h his wife\, pianist Haruko Murphy. Together they share a vibrant perform
 ing partnership and a joyful family life as devoted parents to six childr
 en.<br /><br />  Born in Japan\, <strong>Haruko Murphy</strong> moved wit
 h her family to San José\, California\, at age 14. She earned both the B
 achelor of Music and Master of Music degrees in Piano Performance\, each 
 with Highest Distinction\, from the Indiana University Jacobs School of M
 usic\, where she studied under Distinguished Professor Menahem Pressler. 
 She later continued her training at the Sweelinck Conservatorium in Amste
 rdam as a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinning solo pianist early in her 
 career\, Murphy now enjoys an active life as a collaborative pianist and 
 chamber musician. She specializes in the repertoire of the classical saxo
 phone and has performed in concert with many internationally renowned art
 ists\, including Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arno Bornkamp\, the Masato Kumoi 
 Saxophone Quartet\, and numerous others\, as well as her husband\, Otis M
 urphy. She is a frequent collaborative pianist at the Jacobs School.<br /
 ><br />  Originally from Palisade\, Colorado\, <strong>Paul Lorenz</stron
 g> currently lives in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, where he is visiting assista
 nt professor of saxophone at Purdue University Fort Wayne (PFW). Lorenz i
 s pursuing a Doctor of Music degree in Saxophone Performance at the India
 na University Jacobs School of Music\, where he served as associate instr
 uctor of saxophone. He also earned a Performer Diploma in Solo Performanc
 e at the Jacobs School and holds degrees from the University of Missouri 
 – Kansas City (Master of Music) and the University of Colorado at Bould
 er (Bachelor of Music). His primary teachers include Otis Murphy\, Zachar
 y Shemon\, and Tom Myer. Lorenz is active as both a solo performer and ch
 amber musician\, and also teaches locally in the Fort Wayne area. He was 
 recently featured as a soloist with the PFW Symphonic Wind Ensemble\, as 
 well as at the 2023 IMEA Conference in Fort Wayne\, Indiana. He also join
 ed the ensemble as soloist on their 2023tour through Europe. He was featu
 red as guest soloist with the South Shore Orchestra in the 2024 premiere 
 of a new work by George Kontogiorgos. In addition to his work at PFW\, he
  is the on-call saxophonist for the Fort Wayne Philharmonic Orchestra. Lo
 renz is the alto saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, which was formed at Jacobs
  in 2016. Soma was awarded the Bronze Medal in the Fischoff National Cham
 ber Music Competition (2021)\, Grand Prize in the 9<sup>th</sup> Plowman 
 Chamber Music Competition\, Second Prize (2021)\, and Third Prize (2020) 
 in the Music Teachers National Association Competition\, First Prize in t
 he NOLA Emerging Artists Chamber Competition (2021)\, first runner up in 
 the Classics Alive Young Artist Competition in 2018\, and First Prize in 
 the 2017 Chicago Woodwind Ensemble Competition. Based in Bloomington\, So
 ma has been active since 2016 and has performed a wide variety of concert
 s including recitals at the International Navy Band Symposium and the Nor
 th American Saxophone Alliance and was featured as part of WFIU's NPR bro
 adcast in 2018.</p>
