BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260707T003000Z
DTEND:20260707T020000Z
LOCATION:Auer Hall
SUMMARY:Saxophone Academy Faculty Concert – Paul Lorenz\, saxophone\; A
 ngela Space\, saxophone\; Haruko Murphy\, piano\; with Otis Murphy\, saxo
 phone
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  A. Marcello/Pittel: Oboe Concerto in C Minor\, S.
 Z799Piazzolla/Isoda/Sugawa: Selections from "Histoire du tango" (1985)Bea
 ch/Duncan: Romance\, Op. 23 (1893)Ryota Ishikawa: Blooming Ireland (2013)
 Morricone/Yamaguchi: New Cinema Paradise Medley (1988)Demersseman: Fantai
 sie sur un thème original (1860)About the Artists  Originally from Palis
 ade\, Colorado\, Paul Lorenz currently lives in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, wh
 ere he is visiting assistant professor of saxophone at Purdue University 
 Fort Wayne (PFW). Lorenz is pursuing a Doctor of Music degree in Saxophon
 e Performance at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, where he
  served as associate instructor of saxophone. He also earned a Performer 
 Diploma in Solo Performance at the Jacobs School and holds degrees from t
 he University of Missouri – Kansas City (Master of Music) and the Unive
 rsity of Colorado at Boulder (Bachelor of Music). His primary teachers in
 clude Otis Murphy\, Zachary Shemon\, and Tom Myer. Lorenz is active as bo
 th a solo performer and chamber musician\, and also teaches locally in th
 e Fort Wayne area. He was recently featured as a soloist with the PFW Sym
 phonic Wind Ensemble\, as well as at the 2023 IMEA Conference in Fort Way
 ne\, Indiana. He also joined the ensemble as soloist on their 2023tour th
 rough Europe. He was featured as guest soloist with the South Shore Orche
 stra in the 2024 premiere of a new work by George Kontogiorgos. In additi
 on to his work at PFW\, he is the on-call saxophonist for the Fort Wayne 
 Philharmonic Orchestra. Lorenz is the alto saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, 
 which was formed at Jacobs in 2016. Soma was awarded the Bronze Medal in 
 the Fischoff National Chamber Music Competition (2021)\, Grand Prize in t
 he 9th Plowman Chamber Music Competition\, Second Prize (2021)\, and Thir
 d Prize (2020) in the Music Teachers National Association Competition\, F
 irst Prize in the NOLA Emerging Artists Chamber Competition (2021)\, firs
 t runner up in the Classics Alive Young Artist Competition in 2018\, and 
 First Prize in the 2017 Chicago Woodwind Ensemble Competition. Based in B
 loomington\, Soma has been active since 2016 and has performed a wide var
 iety of concerts including recitals at the International Navy Band Sympos
 ium and the North American Saxophone Alliance and was featured as part of
  WFIU's NPR broadcast in 2018.Angela Space is a classically trained music
 ian and artist. Born in Hartford\, she grew up in Windsor\, Connecticut\,
  and spent the majority of her adult life in Oxford\, England. She has ha
 d a varied career in music. She was a music faculty member at several uni
 versities in the United States and UK with her longest tenure at the Univ
 ersity of Southampton. Outside of academia\, Space has also taught music 
 at Crisis UK\, Headington Rye Oxford School\, and at Her Majesty's Prison
  Springhill. Having earned a Doctor of Musical Arts in Saxophone Performa
 nce\, Space has performed with the South Bend Symphony\, the Florida Phil
 harmonic\, the New World Symphony\, and the Temptations. Under the monike
 r Rainbow Reservoir she recorded two albums on Oddbox Records. Her band p
 erformed in venues and festivals across the UK and has been heard on BBC 
 Radio 3 and 6 and BBC Radio Oxford. As a fine artist\, Space works primar
 ily in oils. She was accepted into the 2025 Hoosier Art Salon and the 202
 3 Indiana Heritage Arts exhibition and has exhibited work with Bloomingto
 n Portrait Group and Upland Plein Air Society. She is a member of IPAPA a
 nd the Portrait Society of America. She has studied with Eric Jacobsen an
 d Dale Zinkowski. Space currently divides her time between her home and s
 tudio in Indiana and her place of solitude in Andalucia Spain.  Born in J
 apan\, Haruko Murphy moved with her family to San José\, California\, at
  age 14. She earned both the Bachelor of Music and Master of Music degree
 s in Piano Performance\, each with Highest Distinction\, from the Indiana
  University Jacobs School of Music\, where she studied under Distinguishe
 d Professor Menahem Pressler. She later continued her training at the Swe
 elinck Conservatorium in Amsterdam as a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinn
 ing solo pianist early in her career\, Murphy now enjoys an active life a
 s a collaborative pianist and chamber musician. She specializes in the re
 pertoire of the classical saxophone and has performed in concert with man
 y internationally renowned artists\, including Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arn
 o Bornkamp\, the Masato Kumoi Saxophone Quartet\, and numerous others\, a
 s well as her husband\, Otis Murphy. She is a frequent collaborative pian
 ist at the Jacobs School.Otis Murphy is professor of saxophone at the Ind
 iana University Jacobs School of Music\, where he joined the faculty at a
 ge 28\, becoming one of the youngest professors in the school's history. 
 Recognized internationally for his artistry\, he has performed in more th
 an 25 countries across four continents\, appearing on distinguished stage
 s including Carnegie Hall and Merkin Hall in New York\, Casals Hall in To
 kyo\, and the Palau de la Música in Valencia. Murphy is a laureate in se
 veral national and international competitions\, including the Adolphe Sax
  International Saxophone Competition and the Jean‑Marie Londeix Interna
 tional Saxophone Competition. His discography includes numerous acclaimed
  recordings\, most notably his contribution to Pacifica Quartet's Contemp
 orary Voices\, featuring Ellen Taaffe Zwilich's Quintet for Alto Saxophon
 e and String Quartet\, which won a Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music/Sm
 all Ensemble Performance. Internationally respected as a pedagogue\, Murp
 hy has led guest teaching engagements and master classes at major institu
 tions such as the Paris Conservatory\, the Amsterdam Conservatory\, the C
 ologne Academy of Music\, the Royal College of Music in London\, and the 
 Hamamatsu International Wind Instrument Academy. His students have garner
 ed significant recognition\, earning awards in major competitions and hol
 ding professional appointments worldwide as performers\, educators\, and 
 members of premier military ensembles. Murphy lives in Bloomington\, Indi
 ana\, with his wife\, pianist Haruko Murphy. Together they share a vibran
 t performing partnership and a joyful family life as devoted parents to s
 ix children.
