 cello/Pittel: <em>Oboe Concerto in C Minor\, S.Z799<br /></em>Piazzolla/I
 soda/Sugawa: <em>Selections from "Histoire du tango" (1985)<br /></em>Bea
 ch/Duncan: <em>Romance\, Op. 23 (1893)<br /></em>Ryota Ishikawa: <em>Bloo
 ming Ireland (2013)<br /></em>Morricone/Yamaguchi: <em>New Cinema Paradis
 e Medley (1988)<br /></em>Demersseman: <em>Fantaisie sur un thème origin
 al (1860)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br /> 
  Originally from Palisade\, Colorado\, <strong>Paul Lorenz</strong> curre
 ntly lives in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, where he is visiting assistant profe
 ssor of saxophone at Purdue University Fort Wayne (PFW). Lorenz is pursui
 ng a Doctor of Music degree in Saxophone Performance at the Indiana Unive
 rsity Jacobs School of Music\, where he served as associate instructor of
  saxophone. He also earned a Performer Diploma in Solo Performance at the
  Jacobs School and holds degrees from the University of Missouri – Kans
 as City (Master of Music) and the University of Colorado at Boulder (Bach
 elor of Music). His primary teachers include Otis Murphy\, Zachary Shemon
 \, and Tom Myer. Lorenz is active as both a solo performer and chamber mu
 sician\, and also teaches locally in the Fort Wayne area. He was recently
  featured as a soloist with the PFW Symphonic Wind Ensemble\, as well as 
 at the 2023 IMEA Conference in Fort Wayne\, Indiana. He also joined the e
 nsemble as soloist on their 2023tour through Europe. He was featured as g
 uest soloist with the South Shore Orchestra in the 2024 premiere of a new
  work by George Kontogiorgos. In addition to his work at PFW\, he is the 
 on-call saxophonist for the Fort Wayne Philharmonic Orchestra. Lorenz is 
 the alto saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, which was formed at Jacobs in 2016
 . Soma was awarded the Bronze Medal in the Fischoff National Chamber Musi
 c Competition (2021)\, Grand Prize in the 9<sup>th</sup> Plowman Chamber 
 Music Competition\, Second Prize (2021)\, and Third Prize (2020) in the M
 usic Teachers National Association Competition\, First Prize in the NOLA 
 Emerging Artists Chamber Competition (2021)\, first runner up in the Clas
 sics Alive Young Artist Competition in 2018\, and First Prize in the 2017
  Chicago Woodwind Ensemble Competition. Based in Bloomington\, Soma has b
 een active since 2016 and has performed a wide variety of concerts includ
 ing recitals at the International Navy Band Symposium and the North Ameri
 can Saxophone Alliance and was featured as part of WFIU's NPR broadcast i
 n 2018.<br /><br /><strong>Angela Space</strong> is a classically trained
  musician and artist. Born in Hartford\, she grew up in Windsor\, Connect
 icut\, and spent the majority of her adult life in Oxford\, England. She 
 has had a varied career in music. She was a music faculty member at sever
 al universities in the United States and UK with her longest tenure at th
 e University of Southampton. Outside of academia\, Space has also taught 
 music at Crisis UK\, Headington Rye Oxford School\, and at Her Majesty's 
 Prison Springhill. Having earned a Doctor of Musical Arts in Saxophone Pe
 rformance\, Space has performed with the South Bend Symphony\, the Florid
 a Philharmonic\, the New World Symphony\, and the Temptations. Under the 
 moniker Rainbow Reservoir she recorded two albums on Oddbox Records. Her 
 band performed in venues and festivals across the UK and has been heard o
 n BBC Radio 3 and 6 and BBC Radio Oxford. As a fine artist\, Space works 
 primarily in oils. She was accepted into the 2025 Hoosier Art Salon and t
 he 2023 Indiana Heritage Arts exhibition and has exhibited work with Bloo
 mington Portrait Group and Upland Plein Air Society. She is a member of I
 PAPA and the Portrait Society of America. She has studied with Eric Jacob
 sen and Dale Zinkowski. Space currently divides her time between her home
  and studio in Indiana and her place of solitude in Andalucia Spain.<br /
 ><br />  Born in Japan\, <strong>Haruko Murphy</strong> moved with her fa
 mily to San José\, California\, at age 14. She earned both the Bachelor 
 of Music and Master of Music degrees in Piano Performance\, each with Hig
 hest Distinction\, from the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, w
 here she studied under Distinguished Professor Menahem Pressler. She late
 r continued her training at the Sweelinck Conservatorium in Amsterdam as 
 a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinning solo pianist early in her career\,
  Murphy now enjoys an active life as a collaborative pianist and chamber 
 musician. She specializes in the repertoire of the classical saxophone an
 d has performed in concert with many internationally renowned artists\, i
 ncluding Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arno Bornkamp\, the Masato Kumoi Saxophon
 e Quartet\, and numerous others\, as well as her husband\, Otis Murphy. S
 he is a frequent collaborative pianist at the Jacobs School.<br /><br /><
 strong>Otis Murphy</strong> is professor of saxophone at the Indiana Univ
 ersity Jacobs School of Music\, where he joined the faculty at age 28\, b
 ecoming one of the youngest professors in the school's history. Recognize
 d internationally for his artistry\, he has performed in more than 25 cou
 ntries across four continents\, appearing on distinguished stages includi
 ng Carnegie Hall and Merkin Hall in New York\, Casals Hall in Tokyo\, and
  the Palau de la Música in Valencia. Murphy is a laureate in several nat
 ional and international competitions\, including the Adolphe Sax Internat
 ional Saxophone Competition and the Jean‑Marie Londeix International Sa
 xophone Competition. His discography includes numerous acclaimed recordin
 gs\, most notably his contribution to Pacifica Quartet's <em>Contemporary
  Voices</em>\, featuring Ellen Taaffe Zwilich's Quintet for Alto Saxophon
 e and String Quartet\, which won a Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music/Sm
 all Ensemble Performance. Internationally respected as a pedagogue\, Murp
 hy has led guest teaching engagements and master classes at major institu
 tions such as the Paris Conservatory\, the Amsterdam Conservatory\, the C
 ologne Academy of Music\, the Royal College of Music in London\, and the 
 Hamamatsu International Wind Instrument Academy. His students have garner
 ed significant recognition\, earning awards in major competitions and hol
 ding professional appointments worldwide as performers\, educators\, and 
 members of premier military ensembles. Murphy lives in Bloomington\, Indi
 ana\, with his wife\, pianist Haruko Murphy. Together they share a vibran
 t performing partnership and a joyful family life as devoted parents to s
 ix children.</p>
